          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is more complex than simple \"wear and tear\" because it's an active disorder involving the entire joint, not just a passive breakdown of cartilage. It affects the cartilage, the bone beneath it, ligaments, the joint lining, and surrounding muscles.\n\nThe underlying pathology involves progressive cartilage loss, and the bone underneath actively remodels in response. This process includes inflammation and the body's attempts at repair, rather than just mechanical erosion. In fact, resting the joint, as one might do for simple wear, can worsen the condition due to muscle weakness, which is why exercise is a primary treatment.\n\nThis information comes from the \"Osteoarthritis\" document, categorized as a general clinical reference.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
